 choose requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction."}}},{"id":"cedf763f-ba46-5e1e-aa04-6ba17aee28b7","topicSlug":"verb-patterns","level":"A2","prompt":"I prepare ____ the research findings. (present)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"presenting"},{"id":"B","text":"to presenting"},{"id":"C","text":"to present"},{"id":"D","text":"present"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Want, hope, plan, decide and several other verbs take to plus the base form when followed directly by another verb. Focus on **complement form** in this question.","explanation":"Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"complement-form","contextKey":"complement-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-patterns"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"bbea8e1256838e8290ef773f548996f83397b646a106076374c309dc2cac6045","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **complement form**. Want, hope, plan, decide and several other verbs take to plus the base form when followed directly by another verb.","Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings.","Choose **to present**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings.","B":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **to presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings.","C":"Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings.","D":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **present** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Prepare takes to plus the base verb here: prepare to present the research findings."}}},{"id":"7712d8fc-58d6-5e47-af52-5a1634134c22","topicSlug":"verb-patterns","level":"A2","prompt":"Do you prepare ____ the research findings? (present)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"present"},{"id":"B","text":"presenting"},{"id":"C","text":"to presenting"},{"id":"D","text":"to present"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"A question or negative does not change the complement pattern. Some verbs, including like and love, allow both forms and are excluded from single-answer contrasts here. Focus on **question pattern** in this question.","explanation":"Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"question-pattern","contextKey":"complement-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-patterns"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"f474b9cba6bf80e0047534c334c39e3eb62a74467ce469015d03f66883408f55","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **question pattern**. A question or negative does not change the complement pattern. Some verbs, including like and love, allow both forms and are excluded from single-answer contrasts here.","Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present.","Choose **to present**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **present** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present.","B":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present.","C":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **to presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present.","D":"Making a question with do does not change the complement: prepare to present."}}},{"id":"4352e6f1-0430-547a-a298-d94968ed506c","topicSlug":"verb-patterns","level":"A2","prompt":"I do not prepare ____ the research findings. (present)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"present"},{"id":"B","text":"presenting"},{"id":"C","text":"to presenting"},{"id":"D","text":"to present"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"A question or negative does not change the complement pattern. Some verbs, including like and love, allow both forms and are excluded from single-answer contrasts here. Focus on **negative pattern** in this question.","explanation":"The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"negative-pattern","contextKey":"complement-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-patterns"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"2fc37e85ad50065cd7967aeb561876c324b56fdce663a14f2910f45308266665","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **negative pattern**. A question or negative does not change the complement pattern. Some verbs, including like and love, allow both forms and are excluded from single-answer contrasts here.","The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present.","Choose **to present**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **present** uses a different complement form from the one required here. The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present.","B":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present.","C":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **to presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present.","D":"The negative do not applies to prepare; its complement remains to present."}}},{"id":"1eb48750-c89a-5a8d-a4e6-567c0093febe","topicSlug":"verb-patterns","level":"A2","prompt":"Correct only the second verb: “I prepare presenting the research findings.” Replace “presenting” with ____.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"presenting"},{"id":"B","text":"to presenting"},{"id":"C","text":"to present"},{"id":"D","text":"present"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Want, hope, plan, decide and several other verbs take to plus the base form when followed directly by another verb. Focus on **correction** in this question.","explanation":"Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"correction","contextKey":"complement-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verb-patterns"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"fd17a3a061c0a71cf47a3e29b4cd58a07da7929470ddedc41e73f595907e62d4","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **correction**. Want, hope, plan, decide and several other verbs take to plus the base form when followed directly by another verb.","Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction.","Choose **to present**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction.","B":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **to presenting** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction.","C":"Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction.","D":"The first verb selects a particular complement. **present** uses a different complement form from the one required here. Use prepare to present; prepare requires the to-infinitive pattern in this construction."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["When one verb follows another, the first verb often determines the second verb’s form. These patterns are lexical: a broad meaning such as liking or planning cannot predict every combination. Learn the verb with its complement and a short sentence."],"rules":[{"title":"Verbs with -ing","explanation":"Enjoy, avoid, finish, practise and several other verbs take an -ing form when followed directly by another verb. Learn the pattern with the first verb.","examples":["We enjoy solving riddles.","I avoid wasting paper.","They **decided to leave** early."],"pattern":"enjoy / avoid / finish + **-ing**","paragraphs":["Enjoy, avoid, finish and keep commonly take an -ing complement. The -ing form names the activity and does not itself mark present continuous. I enjoyed swimming is past simple for enjoyed, with an -ing complement naming what was enjoyed."]},{"title":"Verbs with to","explanation":"Want, hope, plan, decide and several other verbs take to plus the base form when followed directly by another verb.","examples":["We hope to arrive early.","I offered to wash up.","She **avoids driving** at night."],"pattern":"want / decide / hope + **to + base verb**","paragraphs":["Want, decide, hope and plan commonly take a to-infinitive. The tense belongs on the first verb: she decided to leave. The second verb stays base after to. Some verbs require a person object as well, such as remind someone to call."]},{"title":"Preserve the pattern","explanation":"A question or negative does not change the complement pattern. Some verbs, including like and love, allow both forms and are excluded from single-answer contrasts here.","examples":["Do you enjoy solving riddles?","I do not want to leave yet.","**Did** you **finish writing** the report?"],"pattern":"**Do** you enjoy **reading**?","paragraphs":["Questions and negatives change the first finite verb as needed but normally preserve the complement pattern: Does she enjoy reading? She doesn’t enjoy reading. Do-support does not turn every later verb into a bare infinitive."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Some verbs allow both patterns with similar meanings, such as like in many contexts. Others change meaning: remember to call is a duty, while remember calling is a memory. Prepositions take -ing, including prepositional to in look forward to meeting. The following chapters separate those contrasts.","examples":["They **decided to leave** early.","She **avoids driving** at night.","**Did** you **finish writing** the report?"]}],"recap":["enjoy / avoid / finish + **-ing**","want / decide / hope + **to + base verb**","**Do** you enjoy **reading**?"]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["infinitive-of-purpose"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
